WEBVTT

NOTE AI-generated fictional adult narrator and native voice. Technical portfolio film, not the NASA demo.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Two headlines, one city, rainfall is rising,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:05.656
rainfall is falling.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.258
Both came from the same NASA record.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.366
The difference was the dates.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.840
Over the most recent 15, it points down,

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.278
with an interval that includes zero.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.171
Neither chart proves a permanent reversal.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.731
So the engineering question became how do we make selective choices visible?

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.560
We began with the data.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.600
NASA power provides monthly data from a reanalysis model,

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.879
not a neighborhood weather station.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.469
Temperature uses a weighted yearly average.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.940
Rainfall arrives as millimeters per day,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.019
so each rate is multiplied by the days in its month.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.965
Incomplete years are left out.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.966
The original response, retrieval date, and source hash travel with the analysis.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.970
A polished graph is useful only if its inputs are inspectable.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.500
Then we compared methods.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.138
Ordinary regression gives an understandable baseline.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.827
Adjusted intervals examine correlated errors.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.620
A robust slope checks unusual years.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.200
We tested the candidates on 20,000 synthetic records.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.720
Under strong dependence, none of the deployable candidates passed every screening case.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.580
So we kept the alternatives visible,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.020
documented their failures, and avoided presenting one interval as a universal fix.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.970
This is exploration, not forecasting.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.300
The next weakness was date selection.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.019
Four examples help, but they can still hide choices.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.792
The map shows every eligible date window.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.180
Start year on one axis, end year on the other.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.542
Each cell shows its fitted rate.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.513
Select a cell, inspect its observations,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.599
and compare it with another period.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.600
These overlapping estimates are not independent votes,

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.970
probabilities, or proof of a climate reversal.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.588
Finally, the result has to survive scrutiny.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.380
Save an investigation with its source response,

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.224
settings, and code fingerprint.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:01.058
Automated tests check calculations and failure paths.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:05.420
An independent reader can inspect the same inputs without trusting a screenshot.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.675
Climate Explorer is still a prototype.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.607
The goal is a climate claim that another person can challenge.
